dict(task="1.3",
stem="An online travel agency is moving its booking database to AWS, keeping the same database engine. Bookings arrive around the clock, and the business can accept only a few minutes of downtime at the switch-over. Which approach best meets this?",
options=["Take a full backup on Friday night, copy it to AWS, and restore it before Monday",
 "Use AWS DMS to copy the database while it keeps running, keep replicating new changes, and switch over once the copy has caught up",
 "Rebuild the bookings as items in Amazon DynamoDB, then switch over",
 "Pause bookings and copy the database files with AWS DataSync"], answer="B",
expl="Database replication is the migration approach for minimal downtime: DMS copies the data while the source stays in use, then keeps applying new changes until the switch-over, so customers keep booking. A backup and restore is tempting because it's simple, but bookings made after the backup would be missing unless the site closed during the copy. Rebuilding on DynamoDB is a redesign, and DataSync moves files, not a live database's changes.",
links=[(L_7R, "Database replication is a technique, used inside a strategy")]),
