dict(task="4.1",
stem="An analytics team creates a 2 TiB Amazon EBS volume for a database that holds only 200 GiB of data so far. It also keeps 200 GiB of files in Amazon S3 Standard. How is each one billed for storage?",
options=["Both are billed for the 200 GiB actually stored",
 "The EBS volume for its full 2 TiB, whether or not it is filled; S3 for the 200 GiB stored",
 "The EBS volume for the data stored on it; S3 for a fixed bucket size chosen at creation",
 "Neither is billed for storage while nobody reads the data"], answer="B",
expl="EBS bills for what you provision, the volume's size, however much of it you fill, so it behaves like a small fixed cost; S3 bills for what you store, plus requests and data transferred out. Billing both for the data stored is tempting because that's how S3 and EFS work, but an EBS volume is reserved capacity. S3 buckets have no size to choose, and stored data is billed whether or not anyone reads it.",
links=[("05-storing-data/01-block-file-object.md", "What you pay for")]),

