dict(task="2.3",
stem="A family-owned winery learns that the email address of its AWS account's root user is the personal mailbox of an employee who left last year, and that the root user's only MFA device was that employee's phone. Which TWO actions best restore the winery's protection of the root user? (Select TWO.)",
options=["Change the root user's email address to a mailbox the winery controls",
 "Give the former employee an IAM user so she can pass on any root user emails",
 "Register a new MFA device for the root user, such as a security key or passkey kept by the winery",
 "Create an IAM user named root with the AdministratorAccess policy",
 "Rely on AWS Shield Standard, which protects every account's sign-in"], answer="AC",
expl="Root protection includes keeping the account's recovery paths, such as its email address, under the company's control, and having working MFA on the root user; changing the root email is itself a root-only task. Giving the former employee an IAM user is tempting as a quick fix, but it leaves the account's recovery in the hands of someone who has left. An IAM user named root is just another IAM user, and Shield Standard protects against DDoS attacks, not sign-ins.",
links=[(L_ID, "Protecting the root user is your job, and you've already done it")]),

